{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 3, "model_name": "mistralai/mixtral-8x7b-instruct-v0.1", "content": " Raymond III of Tripoli was a Count of Tripoli in the Crusader states in the Levant He was born around 1140 and died in 1187 He was the son of Raymond II of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem He became Count of Tripoli in 1152 after his father's death when he was around 12 years old\n\nUnder Raymond III's rule Tripoli became a vassal state of the Kingdom of Jerusalem but Raymond III still maintained a significant amount of independence He was involved in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem and often found himself at odds with the kings of Jerusalem particularly with Amalric I and Baldwin IV\n\nRaymond III was married twice First to Eschiva of Saint Omer and then to Eschiva of Bures Both of his wives were heiresses to important lordships in the Crusader states and Raymond III's marriages to them helped to strengthen his own position\n\nRaymond III played a significant role in the defense of the Crusader states during the Second Crusade He led the defense of Tripoli against the forces of Nur ad-Din and later fought against Saladin at the Battle of Hattin in 1187\n\n*Battle of Hattin*\nAt the Battle of Hattin Raymond III commanded the rear guard of the Crusader army under the overall command of Guy of Lusignan the King of Jerusalem The Crusader army was defeated and Raymond III was captured by Saladin He spent the rest of his life as a prisoner of Saladin and died in 1187\n\n*Death*\nRaymond III's death marked the end of the direct rule of the Counts of Tripoli in the Crusader states The county was eventually inherited by his sister Melisende who was married to Bohemond III of Antioch The county of Tripoli continued to be a vassal state of the Kingdom of Jerusalem until the end of the Crusader states in the Levant in the late 13th century\n\nOverall Raymond III was a significant figure in the Crusader states in the Levant during the 12th cen